  "help": {
    "topics": [
      {
        "name": "terminals",
        "title": "Terminals",
        "summary": "Terminal emulators, their built-in pane shortcuts, and tmux hints, finder, commands and status.",
        "body": "# Terminals\n\nThis bundled plugin owns Flash's terminal knowledge. It declares which apps are terminal emulators (`terminal_emulators`); Flash applies its terminal rules only to them: a Command chord the emulator would type as text is refused, and pixel wheels are refused. Inside them it is the tmux provider: pane, link and status-bar hints, the `s` letter-search grid, the `@terminals` finder, the `tmux session` / `tmux window` commands, the `flash.plugin.terminals.session`, `window` and `pane` status segments, and the tab and pane commands tmux performs itself, declared as `commands[]` entries under Flash's `tab`, `pane`, `scroll` and `app` topics (closing a window or a pane asks first: those entries carry `confirm`). It also declares the chords emulators bind for built-in commands (`bindings`): `pane next` / `pane previous` (`flash pane next` / `flash pane previous`) send Cmd-] / Cmd-[ in Ghostty and iTerm2 when no tmux client hosts the focused terminal; tmux still owns its panes first. It binds none of the `input` commands, none of the `cursor` motions and none of the `move` arrows (`false` for every emulator): a shell, or vim running in it, would receive Cmd-Left or an arrow as input (Up recalls history), so `h` `j` `k` `l`, the half pages, pages and `gg` / `G` scroll there as they always have. Nor does a terminal get `app cancel` (NORMAL's Escape, which a shell or vim would read), `app select_all` or `window close_all`; `app settings` sends Cmd-Comma only to the emulators that bind it (not Alacritty or WezTerm). Remote tmux over SSH is opt-in through `[plugins.terminals] ssh_hosts`. Add an emulator by declaring it in a plugin of your own.\n"
      }
    ]
  }
